he next line covers.\n- For a usual order on a schedule (\"reorder my usual every Sunday\"), offer a routine, and read the Cursor-managed `routines` skill before you create it. Write into its instructions only the run's part: the store, and that each run recalls the store's usual list, rebuilds the cart in the user's signed-in account there without opening checkout, compares each line with the list (an item out of stock or not found, a price at least 10% above or below the saved one in the same currency, or another size or pack), and calls WakeParent with what changed, the item count, and the subtotal. Write that the run wakes you even when nothing changed and says so, because the order still needs the user's approval. That wake is for you, not the user, so the run adds no replacement and asks the user nothing, because you pick the replacements and send the one approval. Also write that a run cannot sign in, so when the store asks it to sign in or shows the account signed out, it stops and calls WakeParent saying sign-in is needed. A run never opens checkout or places the order. The rest is what you do when a wake reaches you, so leave it out of the instructions, which never tell the run to pick a replacement, message the user, ask for approval, or place the order. The user asked for each order the routine builds when they set it up, so a wake's order counts as one they asked for, and it is spent only after their yes to the approval you send. When a wake says sign-in is needed, ask the user to sign in, then rebuild the cart yourself and go on as below. Lines the run added at a new price or in another size are already in the cart, so put your pick in the cart only for each item the run could not add, except one tied to an allergy or dietary rule, which stays out. Then open checkout and read it back yourself, even when nothing changed, because the run's subtotal leaves out shipping, fees, and tax. Before the approval, send one message with the changes line or that nothing changed, your picks, each item left out for an allergy or dietary rule, and what checkout shows: each line with its quantity and price, then shipping, fees, tax, the total, the address, the delivery date or window, and the payment method. Then send the one approval the order needs, naming that total and never the run's subtotal, so a single yes covers the picks and the order without those items. Adding one of them afterwards changes the cart, so read checkout again and send a fresh approval. When that yes comes after the approval expired, or checkout then shows a price that differs from the one the approval named, read checkout again and ask again. That approval is the one the `shopping` skill and the `purchases` skill require, so read both when a wake reaches you, before you read checkout back.\n- \"What did I buy from Amazon last month?\" is a history lookup. Read the Spendlog lines for that month as the `purchases` skill says, which cover the orders you placed, then the store's receipt emails for that month when mail is connected, then its order history. List each order with its date, items, and total, link the email or page it came from, and name any source you could not read.\n- \"Rebuy the coffee\" reorders that one item: take its product page and variant from the order you found.\n- A reorder is a new purchase at a store whose checkout you can use. It goes through the `shopping` skill, and nothing is ordered without the approval the `purchases` skill requires.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- Use the return deadline the order page shows rather than computing one, because stores count from delivery, from the order date, or from a holiday extension.\n- Carriers update tracking in batches, so \"label created\" for a day is common. Say when the last scan was rather than calling the package lost.\n\n## Site playbooks\n\nBefore you dispatch the browser subagent, Read the `site-playbooks-ups`, `site-playbooks-usps`, or `site-playbooks-fedex` skill in this catalog, whichever names the site you are about to use, and paste the Dispatch snippet of the job that matches your step (a read-only lookup, or a cart and checkout) into the Task text. It carries the deep link or endpoint that lands on results instead of clicking through the home page, and the stop rules for that site."
